================================================================
F1  BLOCKING — P-010's pinned subject is wrong: an off-by-one in the cap
================================================================
Files: adapters/native/src/index.ts (deriveSubject, the cap block)
       packages/cli/src/check.test.ts:405-412 (the comment), :422 (the pin)

The brief pins the twelve output-by-output and states the post-fix P-010
value verbatim:
  "note the 120 for P-010 is the pre-land-7 implementation and must become
   119+`…` under the corrected cap rule."
i.e. 119 characters of text plus the ellipsis = 120 total.

MEASUREMENT of P-010's first paragraph after unwrap + strip (197 chars):
  index 110 " "  111 "("  112 "T"  113 "h"  114 "e"  115 " "
        116 "g"  117 "a"  118 "p"  119 " "  120 "i"  121 "n"
The word "gap" occupies 116-118 and ENDS EXACTLY at the 119-character text
boundary, with a space at index 119. P-010 is the only one of the twelve
where the character at index 119 is a space (censor measured s[119] for all
twelve: "t" "-" "d" "r" "d" "—" "e" "d" " " "d" "g" "-").

  spec value      : 119 chars + "…" = 120 total
    "...not work. (The gap…"          <- does NOT cut mid-word (s[119] is a space)
  builder's pin   : 115 chars + "…" = 116 total
    "...not work. (The…"              <- drops the complete word "gap"

ROOT CAUSE, one line:
    const budget = LIMIT - 1;                 // 119
    let cut = s.slice(0, budget);             // indices 0..118 only
    const lastSpace = cut.lastIndexOf(" ");   // finds 115, never 119
The space AT the boundary (index 119) is outside the search window, so the
last full word before the cap is discarded whenever it ends exactly on the
boundary. The no-space path (the synthetic long-URL row) is correct.

THE BUILDER'S STATED JUSTIFICATION IS FACTUALLY FALSE. check.test.ts:405-412
reads "P-010's length (116, uncapped)" and "P-010's first paragraph ends at
'...not re-litigated here.)' well under the cap, so no truncation applies".
The paragraph is 197 characters and the pinned string itself ends in "…", so
truncation plainly applied. The brief's correction note was right; the
builder's measurement and its comment are both wrong.

CLASS OR SINGLE INSTANCE: one root cause, general in scope (any body whose
word boundary lands exactly on the cap loses a whole word), but exactly ONE
instance among the inputs under test. Searched: all twelve real petitiones
(s[119] measured for each, above) plus behaviour 1's synthetic single-token
row (unaffected). No other instance exists in this opus's fixtures.

FIX AND ITS EXACT BLAST RADIUS (measured, both algorithms run over all twelve):
  search a window of LIMIT (120) chars, not LIMIT-1:
      const w = s.slice(0, LIMIT);
      const sp = w.lastIndexOf(" ");
      s = (sp > 0 ? w.slice(0, sp) : s.slice(0, LIMIT - 1)) + "…";
  -> pins that change: 1 (P-010, 116 -> 120)
  -> synthetic long-token row: identical, still 120
  -> the other eleven pins: byte-identical
So the fix is one line in deriveSubject plus one string in check.test.ts's
PINNED table, plus deleting the false comment at :405-412.

MUTATION CORROBORATION: setting `budget = LIMIT` makes P-010 produce exactly
the brief's value —
  FAIL [W-076 b1] P-010: pinned subject exact
       "...not work. (The gap…"
— and breaks the synthetic row to 121, which is why the fix needs the
no-space fallback rather than the budget change alone.

